		<eira:PURI>http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/FederatedDataCatalogueManagementApplicationService</eira:PURI>
		<dcterms:modified>2023-11-20</dcterms:modified>
		<dcterms:identifier>http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/FederatedDataCatalogueManagementApplicationService</dcterms:identifier>
		<dcterms:title><![CDATA[Federated Data Catalogue Management Application Service]]></dcterms:title>
		<dcterms:type>egovera:FederatedDataCatalogueManagementApplicationService</dcterms:type>
		<skos:definition><![CDATA[Federated Data Catalogue Management refers to the practice of centrally managing and organizing metadata about data assets across multiple data catalogues within an organization. It involves creating a unified view of data assets, their attributes, and relationships, while allowing decentralized control and ownership of individual catalogues.]]></skos:definition>
		<eira:definitionSource><![CDATA[Techopedia]]></eira:definitionSource>
		<eira:definitionSourceReference><![CDATA[https://www.techopedia.com/definition/33133/federated-data-catalog-management]]></eira:definitionSourceReference>
		<skos:example><![CDATA[An organization with multiple business units may have separate data catalogues for each unit. With federated data catalogue management, a central system can integrate metadata from all catalogues, enabling users to search and discover data assets across units. For example, a data analyst in one business unit can easily find and access relevant datasets from another unit's catalogue without needing direct access to that catalogue. This promotes cross-functional collaboration, reduces data silos, and improves overall data governance and utilization.]]></skos:example>
		<skos:note><![CDATA[In a federated data catalogue management approach, each data catalogue retains its autonomy and maintains its own metadata, but a central management system is responsible for aggregating and harmonizing the metadata from all catalogues. This allows users to search, discover, and understand data assets across different catalogues, promoting data sharing, collaboration, and governance. The federated approach enables organizations to leverage existing catalogues, avoid data duplication, and maintain data lineage and provenance. It also facilitates compliance with data regulations and standards by providing a comprehensive view of data assets.]]></skos:note>
		<eira:concept>eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock</eira:concept>
		<eira:view>TVA-Data Management Enablers [Motivation]</eira:view>
		<eira:view>TVA-Dataspace Enablers</eira:view>
		<eira:view>Technical view - application</eira:view>
		<eira:eifLayer>TechnicalApplication</eira:eifLayer>
		<skos:broader>http://data.europa.eu/dr8/DigitalSolutionApplicationService</skos:broader>
	</skos:Concept>
	<skos:Concept rdf:about="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/EULoginApplicationService">
		<skos:notation><![CDATA[EU Login]]></skos:notation>
		<dcterms:identifier>http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/EULoginApplicationService</dcterms:identifier>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en"><![CDATA[EU Login]]></skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:topConceptOf rdf:resource="http://data.europa.eu/dr8" />
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en"><![CDATA[Description: EU Login is an authentication service provided by the European Commission that allows users to securely access a wide range of European Union (EU) online services and applications. It provides a single sign-on mechanism, enabling users to use a single set of credentials to access multiple EU services.

Additional information: EU Login, formerly known as ECAS (European Commission Authentication Service), is a centralized authentication system used by various EU institutions, agencies, and other online services. It ensures secure access to sensitive information and services by verifying the identity of users through a username and password combination or other authentication methods. EU Login supports multi-factor authentication, allowing users to enhance the security of their accounts by using additional verification methods such as mobile app-based codes or hardware tokens. It also provides features like password recovery, account management, and user profile settings. EU Login simplifies the user experience by eliminating the need for separate credentials for each EU service, reducing the administrative burden for both users and service providers.

Example: An example of EU Login in action is when a user wants to access the European Commission's online portal to submit a funding application. Instead of creating a new account, the user can simply use their existing EU Login credentials to authenticate themselves and gain access to the application submission system. This streamlines the process for the user and ensures the security and integrity of the application data.

Publisher: European Commission

Source: https://webgate.ec.europa.eu/cas/help.html

LOST view: TVA-Identification and Access Enablers [Motivation]

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/EULoginApplicationService

EIRA concept: eira:SolutionBuildingBlock

Last modification: 2024-01-28

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/EULoginApplicationService

Name: EU Login Application Service

Interoperability Layer: TechnicalApplication

Realises: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/IdentityMangementApplicationService

]]></skos:definition>
		<egovera:EULoginApplicationService_Properties />
	</skos:Concept>
