	<skos:Concept rdf:about="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/LegislationOnBehaviouralInteroperabilityGrouping">
		<skos:notation><![CDATA[Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (NNBI)]]></skos:notation>
		<dcterms:identifier>http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/LegislationOnBehaviouralInteroperabilityGrouping</dcterms:identifier>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en"><![CDATA[Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (NNBI)]]></skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:topConceptOf rdf:resource="http://data.europa.eu/dr8" />
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en"><![CDATA[Definition: Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (NNBI) is a grouping that covers a range of legal and regulatory frameworks, policies, and guidelines that aim to facilitate and govern the interaction and exchange of information between different systems, applications, and components in a way that they can operate together effectively and efficiently. This catalogue does not contain any specific elements as indicated by the provided JSON.

Source: EIRA Team Research

Additional information: The Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (NNBI) is a theoretical catalogue that would encompass various laws, regulations, and guidelines that are designed to ensure the smooth interaction and exchange of information between different systems, applications, and components. These laws and regulations would aim to ensure that these different entities can work together in a seamless and efficient manner, without any barriers or obstacles. This would involve setting standards for data exchange, defining protocols for communication, and establishing guidelines for system design and operation. However, the provided JSON indicates that this catalogue does not currently contain any specific elements.

Example: An example of a legislation that could be included in the Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (NNBI) is a law that sets standards for data exchange between different healthcare systems, ensuring that patient information can be shared seamlessly and securely between different healthcare providers. Another example could be a regulation that defines the protocols for communication between different components of a smart city, ensuring that they can work together to optimize city operations and services.

LOST view: LV-Architecture Principles

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/LegislationOnBehaviouralInteroperabilityGrouping

