		<skos:definition><![CDATA[National Binding Instruments (NBI) is a grouping that covers a broad range of legislative and regulatory frameworks related to interoperability in various domains. This includes structural, behavioural, and governance interoperability. The NBI provides a structured approach to the implementation and management of these frameworks, ensuring that they are in line with national and international standards and best practices.]]></skos:definition>
		<eira:definitionSource><![CDATA[EIRA Team Research]]></eira:definitionSource>
		<eira:definitionSourceReference><![CDATA[]]></eira:definitionSourceReference>
		<skos:example><![CDATA[For instance, under the Legislation on Structural Interoperability (NBI), there are specific elements such as the National Schema on eIdentification which provides guidelines on electronic identification processes. Similarly, the National Schema on Cybersecurity outlines the legislative framework for managing cybersecurity risks. The National Schema on eDelivery provides the regulatory framework for electronic delivery services, while the National Schema on the access to Base Registries outlines the guidelines for accessing base registries. These elements provide a structured approach to implementing and managing interoperability in their respective domains.]]></skos:example>
		<skos:note><![CDATA[The National Binding Instruments (NBI) catalogue is a comprehensive repository of legislative and regulatory frameworks that govern interoperability in various domains. It includes the Legislation on Structural Interoperability (NBI), which covers the National Schema on eIdentification, Cybersecurity, eDelivery, and access to Base Registries. It also includes the Legislation on Behavioural Interoperability (NBI) and the Legislation on Governance Interoperability (NBI). Each of these categories contains specific elements that provide detailed information on the respective legislative and regulatory frameworks. The NBI catalogue is designed to facilitate the implementation and management of these frameworks, ensuring compliance with national and international standards and best practices.]]></skos:note>
		<eira:concept>eira:ArchitectureBuildingBlock</eira:concept>
		<eira:view>LV-Architecture Principles</eira:view>
		<eira:view>LV-Binding Power and Jurisdiction</eira:view>
		<eira:eifLayer>Legal</eira:eifLayer>
	</skos:Concept>
	<skos:Concept rdf:about="http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/LegislationOnGovernanceInteroperabilityGrouping">
		<skos:notation><![CDATA[Legislation on Governance Interoperability (NNBI)]]></skos:notation>
		<dcterms:identifier>http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/LegislationOnGovernanceInteroperabilityGrouping</dcterms:identifier>
		<skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en"><![CDATA[Legislation on Governance Interoperability (NNBI)]]></skos:prefLabel>
		<skos:topConceptOf rdf:resource="http://data.europa.eu/dr8" />
		<skos:definition xml:lang="en"><![CDATA[Definition: Legislation on Governance Interoperability (NNBI) is a grouping that covers a comprehensive catalogue of information entities related to laws, regulations, and policies that facilitate the interaction between different governance systems. This catalogue is empty as indicated by the JSON provided, but it is intended to house elements that define and describe the legal and regulatory framework that enables interoperability in governance.

Source: EIRA Team Research

Additional information: The Legislation on Governance Interoperability (NNBI) catalogue is designed to be a repository of information entities that detail the legal and regulatory aspects of governance interoperability. Interoperability in this context refers to the ability of different governance systems to work together, exchange information and function effectively. The catalogue is currently empty, but it is intended to include elements such as specific laws, regulations, policies, guidelines, and standards that facilitate interoperability in governance. Each element in the catalogue will have a name and a description, providing a clear understanding of the element's role and relevance in promoting governance interoperability.

Example: An example of an element that might be included in the Legislation on Governance Interoperability (NNBI) catalogue could be a law like the 'Data Sharing and Governance Act'. The name of the element would be 'Data Sharing and Governance Act' and the description might detail how this law facilitates the sharing of data between different governmental departments, thereby promoting interoperability.

LOST view: LV-Architecture Principles

Identifier: http://data.europa.eu/dr8/egovera/LegislationOnGovernanceInteroperabilityGrouping

